# 9. DYNAMIC COUNTRY AND CITY FARE CALCULATION

Implement an international pricing engine.

The system must automatically identify the operating country and city using the pickup GPS location.

Do not use IP address, phone language, or payment-card country as the primary location for fare selection.

### A. Automatic location and currency detection

Use reverse geocoding or an equivalent location service to identify:

- Country.
- Country ISO code.
- City.
- Operating area.
- Local currency.

Automatically select the appropriate currency.

Examples:

| Country | Currency | Code |
|---|---|---|
| Egypt | Egyptian Pound | EGP |
| Saudi Arabia | Saudi Riyal | SAR |
| UAE | UAE Dirham | AED |
| United Kingdom | Pound Sterling | GBP |
| United States | US Dollar | USD |
| Germany | Euro | EUR |

The system must support additional countries without requiring changes to core pricing logic.

### B. Configurable pricing profiles

Create a country/city pricing configuration containing:

- Country code.
- Country name.
- City.
- Currency code.
- Currency symbol.
- Currency decimal places.
- Service category.
- Base fare.
- Included distance.
- Price per kilometre.
- Waiting charge.
- Minimum fare.
- Booking fee.
- Tax percentage.
- Airport surcharge.
- Night surcharge.
- Traffic surcharge.
- Surge multiplier.
- Effective start date.
- Effective end date.
- Pricing version.
- Active/inactive status.

### C. Egypt initial pricing configuration

Configure Egypt as the initial operating market.

Use the following requested commercial pricing ranges as administrator-configurable starting values:

| Pricing Component | Configuration Range |
|---|---:|
| Base fare | EGP 8.50–20.00 |
| Included distance | First 1 km |
| Additional kilometre | EGP 4.00–10.00 |
| Waiting charge | Configurable by city/district |

These are configurable commercial starting ranges, not automatically verified official tariffs.

Maintain a separate reference profile for applicable local taxi tariffs. Do not automatically assume that public taxi-meter tariffs govern VeloRide's private ride-hailing prices.

Administrators must be able to update pricing when local regulations or commercial requirements change.

### D. Fare calculation

Use actual road-route distance and applicable waiting time.

Implement:

```text
Billable Distance =
MAX(0, Actual Route Distance - Included Distance)

Distance Charge =
Billable Distance × Price Per KM

Subtotal =
Base Fare
+ Distance Charge
+ Waiting Charge
+ Booking Fee
+ Applicable Surcharges

Fare Before Discounts =
Subtotal × Applicable Pricing Multipliers

Final Fare =
Fare Before Discounts
+ Applicable Taxes
- Discounts
```

Apply the configured minimum fare.

Do not calculate commercial trip distance using straight-line GPS distance when road-routing data is available.

### E. Fare estimate response

Every fare estimate must return:

- Country.
- City.
- Currency.
- Currency symbol.
- Pickup location.
- Destination.
- Estimated distance.
- Estimated duration.
- Base fare.
- Distance charge.
- Waiting charge.
- Surcharges.
- Taxes.
- Discounts.
- Final estimated fare.
- Pricing version.
- Quote creation timestamp.
- Quote expiry timestamp.

### F. Pricing consistency

- Save the applicable pricing version when a ride is accepted.
- Do not silently change an accepted fare when an administrator updates pricing.
- Recalculate final trip charges using actual billable distance and waiting time according to the agreed fare policy.
- Record fare adjustments transparently.
- Use appropriate decimal precision and integer minor units for payment processing.
- Never use floating-point arithmetic for financial calculations.

### G. Currency conversion

If platform reporting or settlement uses a different currency:

- Keep the original trip currency.
- Store the conversion rate.
- Store the conversion timestamp.
- Store the conversion source/version.
- Keep original and converted amounts separately.

Do not silently convert the rider's local fare into another currency.

